## 3. Prove nothing changed

Do all of these before you call it done, and report the results.

### 3a. Reassembly check (must be exact)
Write a check that rebuilds the original file from the parts: take the new `index.html`, put the contents of `css/style.css` back between `<style>` and `</style>` where the `<link>` is, and put the 8 JS files, concatenated in load order, back between `<script>` and `</script>` where the `<script src>` tags are. **`diff` the result against the original `index.html` from `git show HEAD:index.html`. It must be empty.** Watch trailing newlines at file boundaries; if the diff only shows newline differences, fix the split, don't ignore them.

Also confirm:
- `git show HEAD:index.html | grep -c ''` line count equals the reassembled line count.
- `grep -ci lumbra` across the repo gives the same total as before (2 in `index.html`, which now live in `js/game.js`, plus those in `max`).

### 3b. Syntax check
Run `node --check` on each `js/*.js` file. They should all pass on their own (they're plain scripts that only reference globals at runtime).

### 3c. Headless run (if a browser is available, e.g. Playwright/Chromium)
Serve the folder (`python3 -m http.server`) and also open it via `file://`. In both cases:
- No console errors and no 404s in the network log.
- The splash shows "SHADOW SKIRR", then the home screen appears after ~2 s.
- `window.__SHADOWSKIRR` and `window.__LUMBRA` both exist and are the same object.
- `typeof SIM, HAZ, LEVELS, HOME, FIXES, DEMO_SOL, MUSIC, BACKDROPS, RENDER` are all defined; `LEVELS.length` is unchanged (30).
- Start level 1 via `__SHADOWSKIRR.startLevel(1)` and let it run a few seconds; no errors.
- The attract/demo playback on the home screen runs (it replays `DEMO_SOL`).
- Seed `localStorage["lumbra.v1"]` with a save in a fresh profile, reload, and confirm it migrates to `shadowskirr.v1` exactly as before.
- Take a screenshot of the splash, the home screen and level 1 at the same viewport size on the **original** `index.html` (from git) and on the new one; they should match.

### 3d. Determinism check (optional but good)
`SIM` is deterministic and is described as shared with a "Node beatability solver" (not in this repo). In Node, load `js/sim.js` + `js/levels.js` into one `vm` context and, for a few levels, `SIM.compile` the level, `SIM.replay` / `SIM.solve` it, and compare the result to the same calls made against the scripts extracted from the original `index.html`. Results must be identical. Do **not** add `module.exports` or any Node-specific code to the game files to make this work; load them with `vm.runInContext` instead.

If any check fails, fix the split. Never change game code to make a check pass.

